From 7bdd136f1cbecc4e6d7d74ec4816bea3ee498196 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thiswillbeyourgithub
 <26625900+thiswillbeyourgithub@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:11:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] voxtral tts: per-request noise seed and euler steps in
 the acoustic decoder

The Gaussian each 12.5 Hz frame's flow-matching ODE starts from is the only
source of randomness in Voxtral's output (the semantic head is an argmax, the
acoustic head is deterministic flow matching, stage 0's sampling params are
inert). Upstream draws it from the global torch RNG with no generator, so a
take is reproducible only as "request N after a fresh boot with requests
1..N-1 identical", and reproducing one specific request means a restart.

Draw it in make_omni_output instead, per request, from a torch.Generator
seeded with SplitMix64(seed, frame index). The frame counter lives in the
request's own extra_args dict, which the model runner hands over by reference,
so frame N depends only on (seed, N). Unlike re-pinning the global RNG this
survives concurrency: each row gets its own stream instead of a share of one
sequence whose split depends on batch composition. With no seeded request in
the batch, nothing is drawn here and behaviour is bit-for-bit upstream.

euler_steps rides the same route into _extract_euler_steps. It is a loop
bound, not a per-row tensor like cfg_alpha, so it is honoured only when every
request in the decode step agrees (warning once otherwise) and it forces the
eager path, since the CUDA graphs bake one fixed step count into their capture.

noise and n_steps are threaded through compute_mm_logits, forward and
decode_one_frame as optional args defaulting to None, so every existing call
site keeps upstream behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
